{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6c0d8466550047ab937858ce0363ed46","ordinal":161,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.161","text":"\n\nWe oppose mythical belief to dogmatic supposition when the poets say that Zeus came down and had sex with mortal women, while the dogmatists consider this impossible; and when the poet says"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ada325aa43e144b1851c9515d28fd665","ordinal":162,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.162","text":"\n\nthat Zeus, out of grief for Sarpedon, “shed drops of blood upon the earth,” while it ⟨is⟩ a belief of philosophers that the divine is free of feeling; and when ⟨......⟩ do away with the myth of the hippocentaurs, offering us the hippocentaur as an example of something that does not exist.\n〔1〕 Sarpedon, a son of Zeus, was killed at Troy; the bloody rain is from Homer, Iliad 16.459."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9e51f4eb34e14a42bfebcf4234ac3059","ordinal":163,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.163","text":"\n\nMany other examples could be taken for each of the oppositions mentioned, but for a brief account these will be enough. At any rate, since this mode too shows so much variation in things, we will not be able to say what the underlying object is like in its nature, only what it appears like relative to this way of life, or this law, or this custom, and so on for each of the others. So by this mode too we must suspend judgment about the nature of external objects. In this way, then, through the Ten Modes, we end up in suspension of judgment."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-008ec094fcc846a598da8d6bfb70b6e4","ordinal":164,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.164","text":"\n\nThe more recent skeptics hand down the following five modes of suspension: first, the one from disagreement; second, the one that throws us into infinite regress; third, the one from relativity; fourth, the one from hypothesis; and fifth, the one from reciprocity.\n〔1〕 Diogenes Laertius attributes the Five Modes to Agrippa, a skeptic probably of the first century AD."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a0c6fc4fdd0744a884306adfe8007d51","ordinal":165,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.165","text":"\n\nThe mode from disagreement is the one by which we find that undecidable dissension about the matter proposed has arisen both in ordinary life and among philosophers. Because of it we are unable either to choose or to reject anything, and we end up in suspension of judgment."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-09c57ff3e4e04d279890f88f1c6529cd","ordinal":166,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.166","text":"\n\nThe mode from infinite regress is the one in which we say that what is offered to confirm the matter proposed needs another confirmation, and that one another, and so on to infinity; so that, since we have no point from which to begin establishing anything, suspension of judgment follows."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-40fc558021a34432b5afe9a30448252c","ordinal":167,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.167","text":"\n\nThe mode from relativity, as we said before, is the one in which the underlying object appears such-and-such relative to what judges and to the things observed along with it, while we suspend judgment about what it is like in its nature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-954726b10d23435c8604b3d0beffa263","ordinal":168,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.168","text":"\n\nThe mode from hypothesis occurs when the dogmatists, being driven into an infinite regress, start from something which they do not establish, but which they claim to assume simply and without proof, as a concession."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ba2cbd3d97e043cfb0c788ee1b2e6bb0","ordinal":169,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.169","text":"\n\nThe mode of reciprocity arises when what ought to confirm the matter under investigation itself needs to be made credible by the matter under investigation. Then, being unable to take either in order to establish the other, we suspend judgment about both. That every matter under investigation can be brought under these modes we will show briefly, as follows."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c2e69499ff424d7fb720a8e421f12560","ordinal":170,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.170","text":"\n\nWhat is proposed is either perceptible or thinkable, and whichever it is, there is disagreement about it. Some say that only perceptible things are true, others only thinkable things, and others that some perceptible and some thinkable things are. Will they say, then, that the disagreement is decidable or undecidable? If undecidable, we say that we must suspend judgment; for one cannot make assertions about matters on which there is undecidable disagreement. If decidable, we ask how it is to be decided."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2cfaa735772d4a5fbda258b987f98b80","ordinal":171,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.171","text":"\n\nTake the perceptible, for example (we will base the argument on this first): is it to be decided by something perceptible or by something thinkable? If by something perceptible, then, since it is perceptible things we are investigating, that too will need something else to make it credible. And if that is also perceptible, it in turn will need something else to make it credible, and so on to infinity."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d7f9d128e5904085a36af85c87260b53","ordinal":172,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.172","text":"\n\nIf the perceptible is to be decided by something thinkable, then, since there is disagreement about thinkable things too, this, being thinkable, will also need to be judged and made credible. So how will it be made credible? If by something thinkable, it will fall into infinite regress in the same way. If by something perceptible, then, since something thinkable was adopted to make the perceptible credible, and something perceptible to make the thinkable credible, the mode of reciprocity is brought in."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-94fa8d1c472f4b2fa2d6002aef36ec17","ordinal":173,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.173","text":"\n\nIf, to escape this, the person arguing with us should claim to assume something by concession and without proof in order to prove what follows, the mode from hypothesis will be brought in, and it leads nowhere. For if the person who makes the hypothesis is credible, we will be no less credible if we always hypothesize the opposite. And if what he hypothesizes is true, he makes it suspect by taking it as a hypothesis rather than establishing it; if it is false, the foundation of what is being established will be rotten."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c91fedec97ba438aa5ea97962991ad88","ordinal":174,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.174","text":"\n\nAgain, if hypothesizing achieves anything toward credibility, let him hypothesize the very thing under investigation, and not something else through which he will supposedly establish the matter under discussion. But if it is absurd to hypothesize what is under investigation, it will also be absurd to hypothesize what lies above it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b0503982ae9b40fab24e2270638c8f83","ordinal":175,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.175","text":"\n\nThat all perceptible things are relative is also clear: they are relative to those who perceive them. It is evident, then, that whatever perceptible thing is proposed to us, it is easy to bring it under the Five Modes. We reason in the same way about the thinkable. If it is said that there is undecidable disagreement about it, it will be granted to us that we must suspend judgment about it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-50b14b75ac3447c3aae491b203233335","ordinal":176,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.176","text":"\n\nIf the disagreement is to be decided, then if through something thinkable, we will drive it into infinite regress; if by something perceptible, into reciprocity. For the perceptible, being in turn disputed and unable to be decided through itself because of the infinite regress, will need the thinkable, just as the thinkable needs the perceptible."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3f4976a00ca141fcbddb117ca221e93d","ordinal":177,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.177","text":"\n\nFor these reasons, anyone who assumes something by hypothesis will again be acting absurdly. Thinkable things, moreover, are relative too: they are called thinkable relative to the mind, and if they were by nature such as they are said to be, there would be no disagreement about them. So the thinkable too has been brought under the Five Modes, and therefore we must in every case suspend judgment about the matter proposed. Such, then, are the Five Modes handed down by the more recent skeptics. They set them out not to throw out the Ten Modes, but to expose the rashness of the dogmatists in a more varied way, using these together with those."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-831b8a6148b54b66b0a9fcfdcbb3adf8","ordinal":178,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.178","text":"\n\nThey also hand down two other modes of suspension. Since everything that is apprehended seems to be apprehended either through itself or through something else, they think that, ⟨by pointing out that nothing is apprehended either through itself or through something else⟩, they bring in perplexity about everything. That nothing is apprehended through itself, they say, is clear from the disagreement that has arisen among the natural philosophers about all perceptible and thinkable things, I think—a disagreement which is undecidable, since we cannot use either a perceptible or a thinkable criterion, because anything we adopt is disputed and therefore not credible."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b794e0faf5a44a5bb1a037a500a8510b","ordinal":179,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.179","text":"\n\nAnd for this reason they do not concede that anything is apprehended through something else either. For if that through which something is apprehended must always itself be apprehended through something else, they throw us into the mode of reciprocity or the mode of infinite regress. And if someone wants to take something apprehended through itself as that through which something else is apprehended, he runs up against the fact that nothing is apprehended through itself, for the reasons already given. How what is in dispute could be apprehended ⟨either⟩ from itself or from something else, we are at a loss to say, since the criterion of truth or of apprehension does not appear, and signs, even apart from proof, are overturned, as we will see in what follows. That will be enough to have said about the modes of suspension for now."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-616e7b115d1843068f7ca82c88fe754f","ordinal":180,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.180","text":"\n\nJust as we hand down the modes of suspension, so some set out modes by which, raising difficulties about particular causal explanations, we bring the dogmatists up short, since it is these above all that they pride themselves on. Aenesidemus, for instance, hands down eight modes by which he thinks he can test every dogmatic causal explanation and show it to be faulty."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-09ab48fe56df4b5fafffa215734a58b5","ordinal":181,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.181","text":"\n\nThe first of these, he says, is the one by which the kind of causal explanation that deals with unseen things has no agreed testimony from what appears. The second is the one by which, often, although there is ample scope for explaining the matter under investigation in many ways, some people explain it in one way only."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-73af395eb0c14a25a5ca7b5b648ce227","ordinal":182,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.182","text":"\n\nThe third, the one by which they give causes for things that happen in an orderly way which display no order. The fourth, the one by which, having grasped how things that appear come about, they think they have apprehended how things that do not appear come about—when perhaps unseen things are brought about in the same way as things that appear, and perhaps not in the same way but in a way peculiar to themselves."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5c93bcbc83f3460792e538c5b95e3763","ordinal":183,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.183","text":"\n\nThe fifth, the one by which practically all of them give causal explanations according to their own hypotheses about the elements, and not according to any common and agreed methods. The sixth, the one by which they often admit what fits their own hypotheses, but dismiss what conflicts with them, even though it has equal plausibility."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8273122f09484e0e975b49b6fe7d2e85","ordinal":184,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.184","text":"\n\nThe seventh, the one by which they often give causes that conflict not only with what appears but even with their own hypotheses. The eighth, the one by which, often, when the things that seem to appear and the things being investigated are equally puzzling, they base their teaching about things equally puzzling on things equally puzzling."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-df9d2287e82c42d6afc66887e6c5bb1c","ordinal":185,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.185","text":"\n\nHe says it is not impossible, either, that some people go wrong in their causal explanations by certain mixed modes, which depend on those just mentioned. But perhaps the Five Modes of suspension would also be enough against causal explanations. For a person will propose a cause that either agrees with all the philosophical schools, with skepticism and with what appears, or does not. And it is perhaps not possible for it to agree; for there is disagreement about everything, both what appears and what is non-evident."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7f75a3129dd040bcb59789ca2336b213","ordinal":186,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.186","text":"\n\nIf it does not agree, he will be asked for the cause of this cause too. If he adopts an apparent cause for an apparent one, or a non-evident cause for a non-evident one, he will fall into infinite regress; if he explains them alternately, into reciprocity. And if he comes to a stop somewhere, either he will say that the cause holds as far as what has been said goes, and so bring in relativity, abolishing its being so in its nature; or he will assume something by hypothesis and be stopped short. So it is perhaps possible to expose the rashness of the dogmatists in causal explanations by these modes as well."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2dfc7a7a5e4c44f28c2051447b77b462","ordinal":187,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.187","text":"\n\nWhen we use each of these modes and the modes of suspension, we utter certain phrases that reveal the skeptical disposition and the way we are affected—saying, for example, “no more,” “nothing is to be determined,” and others. So it would be fitting to discuss these next. Let us begin with the phrase “no more.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ba1152bdc5aa4e9c80211f0e0e7c1a70","ordinal":188,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.188","text":"\n\nWe utter this phrase sometimes in the form I gave, and sometimes as “in no way more.” For it is not the case, as some suppose, that we use “no more” in specific investigations and “in no way more” in general ones: we utter “no more” and “in no way more” indifferently, and we will now discuss them as one. This phrase, then, is elliptical. Just as when we say “a double” we mean in effect “a double house,” and when we say “a broad” we mean in effect “a broad road,” so when we say “no more” we mean in effect “no more this than that,\n〔1〕 The Greek text is defective here; editors restore “a double hearth,” that is, a house with two rooms."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d78869aa1e1d45b28c34c08f6b9b99ed","ordinal":189,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.189","text":"\n\nup or down.” Some skeptics, however, take the “no” as a question—⟨in place of⟩ “what more this than that?”, taking the “what” here as asking for a cause—so that what is said is “why this more than that?” It is common to use questions in place of statements, as in “Who among mortals does not know the one who shared Zeus’ bed?”, and statements in place of questions, as in “I am looking for where Dion lives” and “I am asking why one should admire a poet.” And “what” is used in place of “on account of ⟨what⟩” in Menander: “What was I left behind for?” The phrase “no more this than that” also reveals the way we are affected,\n〔1〕 The verse opens Euripides’ Heracles; the one who “shared Zeus’ bed” is Amphitryon, husband of Alcmena, whom Zeus also slept with."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6738665a7be449219ec7286286ea3fe0","ordinal":190,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.190","text":"\n\nin which, because of the equipollence of the opposed things, we end up in equipoise. By “equipollence” we mean ⟨equality⟩ in what appears plausible to us; by “opposed” we mean, in general, conflicting; and by “equipoise” we mean assent to neither side."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2f8b60b16d8848b493f32d78edfb23ac","ordinal":191,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.191","text":"\n\nEven if the phrase “in no way more” displays the character of assent or of denial, we do not use it in that way. We adopt it indifferently and loosely, either in place of a question or in place of saying “I do not know which of these I should assent to and which [to assent to] not.” ⟨For⟩ our aim is to make clear what appears to us, and we are indifferent about the phrase by which we make it clear. We must realize this too: we utter the phrase “in no way more” without affirming that it is certainly true and firm; we say this about it too only as it appears to us."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e68f686ea3db4f22af4e5cc6c537206a","ordinal":192,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.192","text":"\n\nAbout non-assertion we say the following. “Assertion” has two senses, a general and a specific one. In the general sense it is an utterance that posits or denies something—for example, “It is day,” “It is not day”; in the specific sense it is one that only posits, and in this sense negative statements are not called assertions. Non-assertion, then, is refraining from assertion in the general sense, under which, we say, both affirmation and negation fall. So non-assertion is a way we are affected in which we say we neither posit nor reject anything."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-65e84b656d91401a8c44ecab8b5fb6b2","ordinal":193,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.193","text":"\n\nFrom this it is clear that we adopt non-assertion too, not on the assumption that things are by nature such as to produce non-assertion necessarily, but to indicate that we, now, when we utter it, are affected in this way about these matters under investigation. And we must remember this too: what we say we neither posit nor reject is anything said dogmatically about what is non-evident. For we yield to whatever affects us passively and drives us by necessity to assent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b67a526d74c14c3eb65f8282882f430e","ordinal":194,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.194","text":"\n\nWe adopt “perhaps” and “not perhaps,” “possibly” and “not possibly,” “maybe” and “not maybe” in place of “perhaps it is and perhaps it is not,” “possibly it is and possibly it is not,” and “maybe it is and maybe it is not.” So for brevity’s sake we adopt “not possibly” in place of “possibly it is not,” “not maybe” in place of “maybe it is not,” and “not perhaps” in place of “perhaps it is not.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-913dab8dd2eb4e7c84a72cdb1f1ac0c4","ordinal":195,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.195","text":"\n\nHere again we do not fight about words, nor do we investigate whether the phrases mean these things by nature; we adopt them indifferently, as I said. That these phrases indicate non-assertion is evident, I think. Anyone who says “perhaps it is,” at any rate, in effect also posits what seems to conflict with it, “perhaps it is not,” by not affirming that it is. And the same holds for the rest."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4d867078a29b4b26919aec74262b319c","ordinal":196,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.196","text":"\n\nWe adopt “I suspend judgment” in place of “I cannot say which of the things proposed I should believe and which I should disbelieve,” indicating that the things appear to us equal as regards credibility and lack of credibility. Whether they are equal we do not affirm; we say what appears to us about them when it strikes us. And suspension of judgment gets its name from the intellect’s being held back so that it neither posits nor rejects anything, because of the equipollence of the matters under investigation."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ab88b9d0d2444dc387478ad4590b6629","ordinal":197,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.197","text":"\n\nAbout “I determine nothing” we say this. We take determining to be not simply saying something, but putting forward something non-evident with assent. In this sense the skeptic will perhaps be found to determine nothing—not even “I determine nothing” itself. For this is not a dogmatic supposition, that is, assent to something non-evident, but a phrase that indicates the way we are affected. So when the skeptic says “I determine nothing,” what he is saying is: “I am now affected in such a way as neither to posit nor to reject dogmatically any of the things that fall under this investigation.” And in saying this he is reporting what appears to him about the matters proposed—not making a confident, dogmatic declaration, but describing how he is affected."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6948e9b5263a43129ffb2fa91d41e322","ordinal":198,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.198","text":"\n\nIndeterminacy, too, is a way the intellect is affected, in which we neither reject nor posit any of the things investigated dogmatically, that is, of the non-evident things. So when the skeptic says “everything is undetermined,” he takes “is” in place of “appears to him”; by “everything” he means not all existing things, but those of the non-evident things investigated by the dogmatists that he has gone through; and by “undetermined” he means not surpassing their opposites, or the things that conflict with them in general, in credibility or lack of credibility."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ee5b14d31da14ebe918c4acb2a20583d","ordinal":199,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.199","text":"\n\nAnd just as someone who says “I walk” is in effect saying “I myself walk,” so someone who says “everything is undetermined” also implies “in our view,” or “as far as I am concerned,” or “as it appears to me.” So what he says amounts to this: “All the things investigated ⟨by the⟩ dogmatists that I have examined appear to me such that none of them seems to me to surpass what conflicts with it in credibility or lack of credibility.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d8a8c2980a3245bdbc28ce048ee49d5f","ordinal":200,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.200","text":"\n\nWe take the same line when we say “everything is inapprehensible.” We interpret “everything” in the same way, and we supply “to me,” so that what is said amounts to this: “All the non-evident things investigated dogmatically that I have examined appear to me inapprehensible.” This is not the statement of someone affirming that the things investigated by the dogmatists are of such a nature as to be inapprehensible, but of someone reporting his own feeling—a feeling in which, he says, “I suppose that up to now I have apprehended none of them, because of the equipollence of the opposites.” Hence all the arguments brought forward to show that the phrase refutes itself seem to me out of tune with what we are reporting."}]}